---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import publicity from "../assets/blog/one-owner-1949-martin-0-18/linda-gayle-country-western-publicity-photo.jpg";
import family from "../assets/blog/one-owner-1949-martin-0-18/linda-gayle-family-with-1949-martin-0-18.jpg";
import front from "../assets/blog/one-owner-1949-martin-0-18/1949-martin-0-18-front.jpg";
import back from "../assets/blog/one-owner-1949-martin-0-18/1949-martin-0-18-back.jpg";
import neckBlock from "../assets/blog/one-owner-1949-martin-0-18/1949-martin-0-18-neck-block-stamp.jpg";

const facts = [
  ["Model", "Martin 0-18"],
  ["Year", "1949"],
  ["Original Owner", "Linda Gayle"],
  ["Home", "Iowa"],
  ["Musical Life", "Touring and local radio"],
  ["Provenance", "One playing owner"],
] as const;

const resources = [
  {
    number: "01",
    title: "Look Up a Martin Serial Number",
    description: "Find the production year and locate the neck-block stamp.",
    href: "/martin-serial-and-model-numbers/",
  },
  {
    number: "02",
    title: "Compare Martin 0, 00, and 000 Models",
    description: "Read the body sizes, styles, construction changes, and authentication points.",
    href: "/post/martin-0-00-000-history-authentication-value-guide/",
  },
  {
    number: "03",
    title: "Understand Vintage Martin Value",
    description: "See how condition, originality, repairs, and provenance affect an appraisal.",
    href: "/post/how-to-determine-the-value-of-your-old-martin-acoustic-guitar/",
  },
  {
    number: "04",
    title: "Read More Guitar Stories",
    description: "Meet more musicians, families, and instruments from the JVG archive.",
    href: "/blog/",
  },
] as const;
---

<article class="m49">
  <section class="m49-hero">
    <div class="m49-hero-copy">
      <a class="m49-eyebrow" href="/category/museum-original-owners/">Museum and Original Owners</a>
      <h1>One Owner 1949 Martin 0-18: Linda Gayle's Iowa Guitar</h1>
      <p class="m49-dek">She toured Iowa, played on local radio, and kept making music long after she left the stage. Her Martin stayed with her through it all.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="m49-hero-photo">
      <Image
        src={publicity}
        alt="Historic publicity photograph of Iowa country western musician Linda Gayle holding an acoustic guitar"
        width={Math.min(publicity.width, 960)}
        priority
      />
      <figcaption>Linda Gayle with her guitar in an early country western publicity photograph.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <div class="m49-body" id="story">
    <p class="m49-lead">I purchased this 1949 Martin 0-18 from the niece of its original owner, Linda Gayle. Linda was a country western musician from Iowa who toured around the state and played regularly on several local radio stations from an early age. She stepped back from performing when she started her family, but she kept playing and singing throughout her life.</p>

    <dl class="m49-facts">
      {facts.map(([label, value]) => <div><dt>{label}</dt><dd>{value}</dd></div>)}
    </dl>

    <h2>A Martin Kept in One Family</h2>
    <p>Linda's niece did more than pass along a guitar. She carried the name, the photographs, and the family account that connect this instrument to the woman who played it. That direct line is what separates a documented one-owner guitar from one with a story assembled after the fact.</p>
    <p>The newer family photograph closes the gap between the early publicity picture and the guitar today. The same compact body shape is there, along with the spruce top, mahogany back and sides, and restrained Style 18 trim. Joe's <a href="/post/martin-0-00-000-history-authentication-value-guide/">guide to vintage Martin 0, 00, and 000 guitars</a> explains how the size number and style number identify this part of the Martin line.</p>

    <figure class="m49-portrait">
      <Image
        src={family}
        alt="Linda Gayle's niece holding the 1949 Martin 0-18 beside its case"
        width={Math.min(family.width, 620)}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Linda Gayle's niece with the 1949 Martin 0-18 before it left the family.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Linda Gayle on Iowa Radio</h2>
    <p>Linda began performing young. She traveled around Iowa as a country western musician and became a regular voice on several local radio stations. The publicity photograph records that part of her life in a way a serial number never could. She looks directly into the camera with a guitar across her lap, ready for the next introduction or song.</p>
    <p>Starting a family changed the public side of her musical life. She stepped away from regular performances, but she did not stop playing or singing. The Martin remained part of her life rather than becoming a forgotten souvenir from her radio years.</p>

    <blockquote>She stepped back from performing when she started her family, but she kept playing and singing throughout her life.</blockquote>

    <h2>The 0-18 She Played for a Lifetime</h2>
    <p>A 0-18 is a concert-size Martin, smaller than a 00 or 000. Style 18 pairs a spruce top with mahogany back and sides and simple appointments built around the instrument rather than decoration. The front photograph shows a well-defined spruce grain, a dark tortoise-pattern pickguard, understated soundhole rings, and the small body that made the model comfortable for singing and radio work.</p>
    <p>The back carries the clearest signs of long use. There are scratches, worn finish, and a broad patch of surface wear where the guitar met its player. Those marks belong to Linda's years with the instrument. They are part of the ownership record, not something that needs a new explanation.</p>

    <div class="m49-diptych">
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={front}
          alt="Front of Linda Gayle's natural 1949 Martin 0-18 resting in a green-lined case"
          width={Math.min(front.width, 1600)}
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Spruce top, compact 0 body, and restrained Style 18 trim.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={back}
          alt="Mahogany back of the 1949 Martin 0-18 showing scratches and finish wear from years of playing"
          width={Math.min(back.width, 1600)}
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>The mahogany back records years of handling and play.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <h2>What the Neck-Block Stamp Confirms</h2>
    <p>Martin placed the model and serial stamps on the neck block inside the soundhole. The close photograph reads 0-18 above the serial number, so the model is recorded on the guitar itself. The serial falls in Martin's 1949 production range. Owners can use Joe's <a href="/martin-serial-and-model-numbers/">Martin serial number lookup and model guide</a> to find the same information on another instrument.</p>
    <p>The stamp dates the guitar, but it does not tell us who tuned it before a radio broadcast, who carried it across Iowa, or why it remained in one family for decades. That is what Linda's photographs and her niece's account add.</p>

    <figure class="m49-stamp">
      <Image
        src={neckBlock}
        alt="Neck-block stamp inside the soundhole of the 1949 Martin showing model 0-18 above the serial number"
        width={Math.min(neckBlock.width, 1066)}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>The neck block is stamped 0-18 above the serial number.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Why One-Owner Provenance Carries Weight</h2>
    <p>Clear provenance gives every mark a place in the story. The back wear came from Linda. The old photograph shows her as a working musician. Her niece kept the connection intact and shared it when the guitar changed hands.</p>
    <p>That record also makes the guitar useful beyond this one story. It gives collectors and players a documented example of a postwar 0-18 that stayed with the same musician through a performing life, family life, and many later years of music at home. More instruments with direct family histories appear in the <a href="/category/museum-original-owners/">JVG Museum and Original Owners archive</a>.</p>

    <section class="m49-resources" aria-labelledby="martin-resources">
      <p class="m49-eyebrow">Explore Joe's Vintage Guitars</p>
      <h2 id="martin-resources">More for Martin Owners</h2>
      <div class="m49-resource-grid">
        {resources.map((resource) => (
          <a class="m49-resource" href={resource.href}>
            <b>{resource.number}</b>
            <strong>{resource.title}</strong>
            <small>{resource.description}</small>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="m49-cta">
      <h2>Have a Vintage Martin With a Story</h2>
      <p>If a Martin has stayed in your family, send Joe clear photographs of the front, back, headstock, neck-block stamp, case, and any old pictures or paperwork. The <a href="/free-appraisal/">free vintage guitar appraisal</a> is a good place to start, or you can read how to <a href="/sell-my-martin-guitar/">sell a vintage Martin directly to Joe</a>.</p>
      <div class="m49-buttons">
        <a class="m49-button m49-button-primary" href="/free-appraisal/">Free Appraisal</a>
        <a class="m49-button" href="/sell-my-martin-guitar/">Sell My Martin</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  .m49 {
    --m49-ink: #1d1b18;
    --m49-cream: #fffdf8;
    --m49-gold: #d29a54;
    --m49-muted: #6f675e;
    --m49-line: #d9d0c1;
    --m49-red: var(--color-brand-rust-dark, #682412);
    background: #fff;
    color: var(--m49-ink);
    font: 17px/1.72 var(--font-sans, "Open Sans", Arial, sans-serif);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  :global(body.martin-1949-story-page > header) {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--color-brand-black, #17130f);
    border-bottom: 5px solid var(--color-brand-brown-tan, #c8a77a);
  }
  .m49 :global(img) { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .m49 figure { margin: 0; }
  .m49 a { color: var(--m49-red); text-underline-offset: .18em; }
  .m49 a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #f0b45f; outline-offset: 4px; }

  .m49-hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .93fr) minmax(0, 1.07fr);
    min-height: 76vh;
    background: var(--color-brand-black, #17130f);
  }
  .m49-hero-copy {
    padding: clamp(58px, 7vw, 102px) clamp(26px, 6vw, 88px);
    align-self: center;
  }
  .m49-eyebrow {
    display: block;
    width: max-content;
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    color: var(--m49-gold) !important;
    font: 600 13px/1.2 var(--font-display, Oswald), sans-serif;
    letter-spacing: .17em;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .m49 h1 {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 0 28px;
    color: #fff;
    font: 600 clamp(44px, 6vw, 82px)/.98 var(--font-display, Oswald), sans-serif;
    letter-spacing: -.025em;
  }
  .m49-dek {
    max-width: 640px;
    margin: 0;
    color: #e2d7c6;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.1vw, 28px);
    line-height: 1.38;
  }
  .m49-hero-photo { position: relative; min-height: 560px; aspect-ratio: 3 / 4; overflow: hidden; background: #221b16; }
  .m49-hero-photo :global(img) { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; object-position: center 46%; }
  .m49 .m49-hero-photo figcaption {
    position: absolute;
    right: 20px;
    bottom: 18px;
    left: 20px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 10px 13px;
    background: rgba(18, 16, 14, .84);
    color: #fff;
    font: 13px/1.4 var(--font-sans, "Open Sans", Arial, sans-serif);
  }

  .m49-body { max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 64px 26px 100px; }
  .m49-body p { margin: 0 0 24px; }
  .m49-lead { color: #3f403f; font-size: 23px; line-height: 1.62; }
  .m49 h2 {
    margin: 76px 0 22px;
    color: var(--m49-red);
    font: 600 clamp(34px, 5vw, 50px)/1.04 var(--font-display, Oswald), sans-serif;
    letter-spacing: -.015em;
  }
  .m49-facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    margin: 44px 0 70px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--m49-line);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--m49-line);
    background: #faf6ef;
  }
  .m49-facts div { padding: 18px 20px; border-right: 1px solid var(--m49-line); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--m49-line); }
  .m49-facts dt { color: var(--color-brand-rust, #a03a1e); font: 600 12px/1.2 var(--font-display, Oswald), sans-serif; letter-spacing: .11em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .m49-facts dd { margin: 7px 0 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.35; }
  .m49-body > figure { margin: 44px 0 54px; }
  .m49 figcaption { margin-top: 11px; color: var(--m49-muted); font: italic 14px/1.45 var(--font-sans, "Open Sans", Arial, sans-serif); }
  .m49-portrait { max-width: 620px; margin: 44px auto 54px !important; padding: 14px; background: #fff; border: 1px solid #d2c4b2; box-shadow: 0 18px 45px rgba(46, 33, 22, .14); }
  .m49-portrait figcaption { padding: 0 4px 2px; }
  .m49-diptych {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 100vw;
    margin: 44px 0 54px 50%;
    padding: 0 24px;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }
  .m49-diptych :global(img) { height: min(60vw, 720px); object-fit: cover; }
  .m49 blockquote {
    margin: 56px 0;
    padding: 0 0 0 25px;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-brand-rust-bright, #be4b25);
    color: var(--m49-red);
    font: 600 30px/1.35 var(--font-display, Oswald), sans-serif;
  }

  .m49-resources { margin-top: 72px; padding-top: 48px; border-top: 1px solid var(--m49-line); }
  .m49-resources h2 { margin-top: 0; }
  .m49-resource-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; border-top: 1px solid var(--m49-line); }
  .m49-resource {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 42px 1fr;
    padding: 24px 16px;
    color: var(--m49-ink) !important;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--m49-line);
  }
  .m49-resource:nth-child(odd) { border-right: 1px solid var(--m49-line); }
  .m49-resource b { color: var(--m49-red); font: 600 14px/1.4 var(--font-display, Oswald), sans-serif; }
  .m49-resource strong { font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.3; }
  .m49-resource small { grid-column: 2; margin-top: 6px; color: var(--m49-muted); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.45; }
  .m49-resource:hover strong { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: .18em; }

  .m49-cta { margin-top: 72px; padding: 42px; background: var(--m49-red); color: #fff; }
  .m49-cta h2 { margin: 0 0 18px; color: #fff; font-size: 36px; }
  .m49-cta p { color: #f1e8da; }
  .m49-cta p a { color: #fff6df; font-weight: 700; text-decoration-color: #d8aa70; text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }
  .m49-cta p a:hover { color: #f4c27f; }
  .m49-buttons { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; margin-top: 25px; }
  .m49-button {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 12px 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-brand-cream, #eedfc0);
    color: #fff !important;
    font: 600 14px/1 var(--font-display, Oswald), sans-serif;
    letter-spacing: .05em;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .m49-button-primary { background: var(--color-brand-cream, #eedfc0); color: var(--m49-red) !important; }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    :global(body.martin-1949-story-page > header) { border-bottom-width: 3px; }
    .m49-hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; min-height: auto; }
    .m49-hero-photo { min-height: 78vh; aspect-ratio: auto; }
    .m49-facts { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
    .m49-diptych { grid-template-columns: 1fr; width: auto; margin: 44px 0 54px; padding: 0; transform: none; }
    .m49-diptych :global(img) { height: auto; }
    .m49-resource-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .m49-resource:nth-child(odd) { border-right: 0; }
    .m49-cta { padding: 30px 24px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 470px) {
    .m49-body { padding-right: 20px; padding-left: 20px; }
    .m49-facts { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
